<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Nassau-Weilburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1344 CE – 1816 CE · capital Weilburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Walramian county of the house of Nassau on the Lahn, whose line later ruled the Duchy of Nassau and inherited Luxembourg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Walramian line of the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> held lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, and a partition in 1344 gave Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-count-of-nassau-weilburg">John I</a> a share centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>. John acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> by marriage, and the family built up scattered territories across the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saar-river">Saar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled small, scattered lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> castle, rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style in the 16th century, and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a>. Repeated partitions and reunions created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">County of Nassau-Saarbrücken</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-usingen">Principality of Nassau-Usingen</a> among other branches, and in the 18th century the Weilburg counts used the title of prince.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> survived the end of the empire by joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>. In 1806 it united with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-usingen">Nassau-Usingen</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen's</a> extinction in 1816 the Weilburg line ruled it alone. Annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866, the family inherited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg</a> in 1890.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-nassau-weilburg.jpg" alt="County of Nassau-Weilburg" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Nassau-Weilbourg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1344 CE – 1816 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weilburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John I, Charles Christian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Nassau-Weilburg, Nassau-Weilburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1580&amp;focus=county-of-nassau-weilburg" aria-label="County of Nassau-Weilburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Nassau-Weilburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5188 680L5178 685M5188 680L5229 694M5188 680L5189 692M5188 680L5195 685"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5178 685h0M5229 694h0M5189 692h0M5195 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5188 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5188 680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Weilburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1580&amp;focus=county-of-nassau-weilburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
